      "content": "I understand the desire for subtlety and privacy. One lesser-known strategy is wearing a very thin, non-corroding type of undergarment that can buffer contact with sensitive areas of the chair, potentially affecting the readings. However, this could still raise suspicions if noticed by coworkers. Wear compression garments that are commonly worn for health or comfort purposes, as they may also reduce the transmission of your physical signals. Additionally, if the chair allows, positioning yourself so engaging with sensitive areas, like where the breathing truly breaks the surface, is lessened might help. However, none of these suggestions are guaranteed to work, and they may potentially change your comfort, which could affect your work experience negatively. It's critical to ensure that any measures you consider don't disrupt workplace dignity or ethical considerations. There's value in open and honest communication with the company to fully understand and mitigate privacy concerns directly.",
